# How a file's lines end, decided here rather than by whoever cloned it.
#
# Without this, line endings are a property of the checkout and not of the
# repository: `core.autocrlf` is `input` on the machine this is written on and
# `true` on a GitHub Windows runner, so the same commit becomes two different
# trees. Between 2026-07-31 and 2026-09-03 that made CI red for sixty runs
# while the local gate passed on every one of those commits, because the checks
# that read source match newlines exactly. `tests/house_style.rs` searches for
# `" {\n"` and the seven copies of `the_body_of` under `src/` search for
# `"\n}\n"`; under CRLF neither matches, the reading returns nothing, and a
# check that reads nothing cannot fail for the reason it was written.
#
# So: text is LF everywhere, in every checkout, on every platform. Nothing in
# this tree needs CRLF. There is no `.bat` or `.cmd`, and Inno Setup,
# PowerShell and Python all read LF on Windows without complaint.
#
# `tests/house_style.rs::test_the_repository_pins_the_line_endings_its_own_checks_depend_on`
# holds this file to that, per kind of file the checks actually read, and
# `test_no_file_this_project_writes_holds_a_carriage_return` notices one that
# arrives some other way.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git neither converts them nor tries to diff them as text. Declared
# rather than left to detection: detection is a heuristic on the first bytes,
# and a wav or an ico that begins with something that reads as text would be
# rewritten by the rule above and silently corrupted.
*.wav binary
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.svg text eol=lf
